instructions = """
# DSiMC Tool

Do you need a quick way to look/explore at your data? Do you want a quick and easy way to analyze your data and make nice looking charts? Try out this tool!

The Data Science in Medicine Club (DSiMC) is creating and maintaining a tool to automate data analytics and the generation of graphs for posters/presentations/publications! This is a demo of what we can offer! If this interests you or your group and would like customized analytics of your specific datasets, please reach out to us at [data_science_in_medicine@med.wayne.edu](mailto:data_science_in_medicine@med.wayne.edu)

#### How do I use this tool?

You can try this tool out with your own dataset or the sample one this tool comes with! **Note:** This tool only takes in csv and excel files.

**First**: *Take a quick look at your data to make sure you are using the correct dataset.*

**Second**: *Choose how you want your data to be analyzed. There are several options and each option had various configurations.*
- "Value Counts": This will count the number of unique values in a column of interest. This method is often used for quickly counting/summarizing categorical data. To use this option, you will need to specify a column of interest. This will them count the unique values of the specified column and create a graph on submit.
- "Histogram": This creates a histogram based on the desired column of interest from your dataset. This option will let you specify the number of bins for your histogram- can be increased/decreased to get the desired split (a  histogram displays numerical data by grouping data into "bins" of equal width). 
    - There is another option to "Layer another Column". This option divides each histogram column into the specific contributions from the column of interest. For example, if you choose "Gender" for "Layer another Column", it will divide each column of the histogram into what percent is male and what is female.
- "Grouped": This is grouped analysis. The best way to explain it is with an example. For example in the sample dataset, if you choose "Gender" for "Select Column to Group", "Age" for "Choose the numerical column of Interest" and "mean" for "Choose stat method", the resulting graph gives you the average age per gender in the dataset. This is a quick and easy to get stats on you dataset based on specific values for a column. 

**Third**: Specify the details of each analytic option based on the description above. Choose a Title and a Theme. Then click *Submit* to get a graph!

**Fourth**: Explore you dataset with the provided graph! If you like the graph and want to download it for a poster/publication/presentation, you can download the graph by clicking the camera icon by hovering on the graph!

##### ***Note: If you use the graphs/analytics generated by this tool for your posters/presentations/publications, please cite this tool! Details on citing this tool are down below.***

---
"""